WEBVTT

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:08.400
Today, I am proud to announce that our

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.240
next New Frontiers mission, Dragonfly

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.240
>> Dragonfly

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.440
>> So, Dragonfly

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.200
>> Go Dragonfly.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.000
>> We're building a drone the size of a car

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.960
and we're going to fly it on a moon.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.440
>> Dragonfly will explore Saturn's largest

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.440
moon

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.520
>> In 2027, Dragonfly will launch on its

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.920
872 million kilometer journey to Titan,

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.080
the most Earth-like moon we've

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.400
discovered so far. By the time

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.720
Dragonfly's baseline mission is over,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.240
we'll have flown more than 100 miles,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.800
which is almost double the total

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.720
distance covered by all of the Mars

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.680
rovers combined.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.280
I'm a co-investigator on the Dragonfly

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.720
mission and I'm really looking forward

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.240
to being able to see Titan through

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.040
Dragonfly's eyes. Titan's been running a

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.080
big experiment and it's time for us to

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.080
go collect the results.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.560
Titan

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.320
is so amazing cuz in a lot of ways it's

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.640
very Earth-like.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.680
It's the only moon in the solar system

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:15.080
that has an atmosphere. It has rain, it

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:17.720
has clouds, it has lakes and rivers and

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:20.200
sand dunes and mountains.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.160
And so, big picture,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.920
Titan has all the things that we think

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.520
are required for life. We think that

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.800
there was a period in Earth's history

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.640
around the time when life originated

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.400
when Earth's atmospheric composition was

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.800
actually probably quite similar to

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.840
Titan's atmospheric composition today.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.000
And so, another one of the questions we

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.000
have is, you know, what was the

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.600
chemistry like on the early Earth? How

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.680
was it related to what's happening on

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.080
Titan today? And what does that tell us

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.040
about the origin of life on Earth? Titan

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.920
is enshrouded in this kind of

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.040
orangey-yellow haze that keeps you from

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.800
being able to see the surface. And so,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.600
it was like, "Okay, we can't see the

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.480
surface. It's the only moon in the solar

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.000
system that has an atmosphere." And now

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.800
we're finding out that there's all of

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.240
these carbon containing molecules in the

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.520
atmosphere that are really, really

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:07.200
interesting. And Titan is so interesting

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.200
that we needed to know more about it.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.440
Titan is one of the best, if not the

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:17.080
best place in the solar system to fly.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.680
If you personally were on Titan and you

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.840
had some kind of wings attached to your

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.280
arms for some reason, if you flapped

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.520
your arms, you would actually be able to

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.080
fly um under your own power. It's

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:29.360
actually It's It's that easy.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.720
And it's a combination of two things.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.920
One thing is that the gravity is pretty

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.440
low on Titan. It's about 1/7 of the

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.480
gravity on Earth. So, that helps um

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.360
because you don't need as much, you

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.200
know, energy to get off of the ground.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.320
But the other reason why it's so easy to

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.920
fly on Titan is that the atmosphere is

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.160
actually more dense than it is at the

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.760
surface of Earth. So, um it would be

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.760
much, much easier. It is much, much

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.200
easier, um I think, to try to figure out

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.880
a concept to fly on Titan versus trying

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.280
to send a rover.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.120
Dragonfly is not a little backyard

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.800
drone. It's about the size of a small

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.360
car. And so, that makes it, you know,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.760
pretty robust to a lot of things that a

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.440
backyard drone would not um

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.959
be robust to. Uh although, if it crashes

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.680
into a tree, um as long as we know that

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:15.320
it crashed into a tree on Titan, we will

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.760
be super excited. We will call that a

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.080
successful mission um cuz we found life

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.239
on Titan accidentally. We just don't

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.000
know that much about the surface. And

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:27.120
so, on the one hand, that's

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.280
scientifically exciting. And on the

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.440
other hand, that's a little scary when

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.080
you're an engineer that's trying to

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:36.600
build a spacecraft where you can't, you

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.600
know, call AAA if something goes wrong.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:42.120
Because of the time delay in light

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.400
travel between Titan and Earth,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.959
Dragonfly's going to have to navigate on

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.480
its own. There isn't going to be

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:48.880
somebody, you know, sitting on Earth

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.880
with a joystick flying

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.120
um Dragonfly. There's no GPS at Titan.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.120
Um there's nothing to tell it where it

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.320
is. Our maps that we have right now are

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.360
not very good. And so, kind of one of

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:01.160
these a little bit like, you know, build

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.120
the plane as you're flying it type

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.200
things um in terms of how we navigate.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:09.080
And basically, the way that it's going

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:12.480
to be doing that is by taking a really,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:14.240
really lot of images.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.400
We're basically, every time we fly,

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.720
we're actually going to fly further than

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.120
where we want to land so that we can do

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.160
reconnaissance aerial imaging of the

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.400
place we think we want to go next. And

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.440
then we'll turn around and go back to

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.160
where we had planned to land and land

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.440
there. Once we tell it, "Okay, you know,

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.800
you're going to go do this thing." It

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.440
will have the whole sequence of what it

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.680
needs to do. It will be able to make

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.520
decisions if something goes wrong. Um,

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.200
and it will decide, "Okay, this is what

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.760
I'm what I'm doing now." Um, and then,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.240
you know, we'll report back and say, you

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.919
know, "Look, Mom, I did it." Um,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.520
hopefully.

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:49.560
Uh, but it definitely there's definitely

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.480
a lot of challenges involved in

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.919
having these big time delays um because

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.720
we don't we can't control anything in

00:04:52.919 --> 00:04:55.720
real time.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.160
We have all these ideas about the

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.720
requirements for life. And big picture,

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:07.240
we tend to think of the requirements of

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.760
life as a source of energy, building

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.760
blocks of life, so like just things that

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.919
are made out of carbon, and some type of

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:15.440
liquid. We think that Titan is a place

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:17.640
that it has the possibility of of having

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.840
um life. Things that we know we have on

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:23.520
Titan are a whole, whole variety of what

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.440
we call organic molecules. So, one of

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.160
the reasons why we're so excited about

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.520
carbon when we're thinking about, you

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.919
know, the search for life and the origin

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.440
of life is that um you know,

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:35.200
selfishly,

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.919
uh life on Earth is based um on a lot of

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.720
carbon-containing molecules. And so,

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:40.640
that's one of the reasons why we're so

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.560
excited about it. But, actually,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.680
atomically, it's very interesting.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.840
Carbon carbon can make four different

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.760
bonds.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:51.560
Um, whereas when you look at something

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.920
like oxygen, which can really only make

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:55.680
one bond or two bonds. If you think

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.280
about your kids' LEGOs, you can only

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.960
build so many things with a little like

