00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:07.240
>> This tiny little moon, 500 km in

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:09.640
diameter, has proved that it can

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:12.480
surprise everybody.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:14.280
Water vapor plumes with organic material

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:17.160
coming out of this tiny little moon, who

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.160
would have thought?

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:22.760
I get asked whether Enceladus is my

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:24.760
favorite moon and at the moment it is,

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:26.640
partly based on the observations that my

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:29.440
team made and the discovery that we made

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:29.440
at Enceladus.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:39.560
So, the Cassini mission was the first

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:41.920
spacecraft mission to orbit around

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:44.040
Saturn and its moons. It was also the

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:46.400
first mission to taste what was in an

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:48.320
extraterrestrial ocean.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:50.400
The mission kept being extended and

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:53.600
extended and so in the end the mission

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:55.680
lasted for 13 years. The discoveries

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:57.920
were pretty radical. We were

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.240
flabbergasted, to be frank. I think for

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:02.920
me the most surprising discovery that

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:04.920
was made was this water vapor plume at

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:07.720
Enceladus which we then followed up with

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:10.360
discoveries about liquid water ocean

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:12.520
under the surface, a heat source,

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:15.520
organic material. I've now given away

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:15.520
all the secrets.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:20.720
My team and I changed the course of the

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:23.280
mission cuz this was a team effort.

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:25.040
So, we had three flybys of Enceladus and

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:27.560
two of these flybys were quite distant

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:29.800
flybys. In the data that my instrument

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:31.840
took, so data from the magnetic field in

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:34.040
the environment of Enceladus, we saw

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:35.800
some strange signatures that we weren't

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:37.440
expecting to see.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:39.840
And what those signatures were telling

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:42.640
us was almost as if Enceladus was a

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.920
bigger obstacle, a bigger body than it

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:46.680
looked. We thought maybe we hadn't got

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.560
the trajectory of the spacecraft back

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:50.480
properly, so maybe we were seeing an

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:52.040
artifact in the data.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:54.240
But the fact that we had saw it on two

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:56.080
separate flybys

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:57.440
meant that we

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:58.920
we said, "Okay, we thought we were

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:01.480
seeing something." So, I actually

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:03.440
traveled out to the Jet Propulsion Lab.

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:05.400
And I was planning to make a

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:07.320
presentation to the project, cuz what I

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.760
wanted to do was I wanted to persuade

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:11.760
them that we thought we were seeing this

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:14.160
really strange atmospheric signature.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:16.440
Um and that it would be really good to

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:19.280
go close on the third flyby.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:21.520
And I was really nervous because if the

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:23.520
flyby changed, that meant all of the

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:25.760
plans that we had made for the last 6

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:27.280
and 1/2 years would have to be changed.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:29.160
And some instruments would not take the

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:32.760
data that they planned to.

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:35.560
But the possibility that we would

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:38.080
discover this water vapor atmosphere of

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:40.480
Enceladus was exciting enough that the

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:43.480
majority of people decided it was worth

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:45.320
a go. It was extremely nerve-racking

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:47.200
waiting for that third flyby. So, you

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:49.640
know, I'd essentially put my and my

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:51.720
team's reputation on the line.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.480
And so, I must confess, the first the

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.880
two or three nights before the flyby, I

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.200
didn't sleep very well. I tossed and

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:58.680
turned a lot.

00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:00.200
Cuz if they hadn't found anything no one

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:01.240
would ever have believed anything I said

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:03.120
again.

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:06.360
Yeah, every once in a while, you have to

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:07.520
be brave, and you have to

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:09.280
say that you think you're seeing

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:11.199
something. But luckily, they found

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:13.040
something.

00:17:11.199 --> 00:17:15.040
When we had that third flyby, what we

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:17.319
found was instead of there being an

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:19.560
atmosphere covering the entire surface,

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:22.480
there was simply an outgassing of water

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:24.280
vapor from the South Pole.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:26.040
In addition to that, because we went so

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:28.360
close, all of the other instruments were

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:30.280
able to take fantastic data sets as

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:32.600
well. And what we found is there were

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:35.600
cracks at the South Pole through which

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:38.760
this water vapor was escaping.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:40.720
There was an internal heat source.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:42.360
There was organic material.

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:44.400
And so that meant we had three of the

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:47.560
four things for

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:48.920
potential habitability to form. You need

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:50.640
a heat source.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:52.480
You need liquid water.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:54.400
You need organic material. And then you

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:56.400
need those first three things to be

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:58.080
stable enough over a long enough period

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:00.680
of time that something can actually

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:03.520
happen. So that's when there was a a

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:05.240
real focus on Enceladus for potentially

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:07.920
being a place where

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:09.600
life or habitability might have been

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:11.680
there in the past or might be able to

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.200
form in the future.

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:15.320
The coming together of all of the

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:18.080
Enceladus data and the discoveries that

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:20.280
were made, um,

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:22.800
underpins what I think is one of the

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:24.440
most important

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:27.000
discoveries that has been made in

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:30.640
planetary science in the last 50 years.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:33.560
And that is that you don't need to look

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:35.400
close to the sun to find liquid water.

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:37.760
You can be quite far from the sun, but

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:39.040
you can find liquid water, just not on

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:40.880
the surface. It's underneath the

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:44.080
surface. And so that's been a almost

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:46.440
been a sea change in planetary science.

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:49.800
We now have a a much better

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:53.800
understanding about Enceladus and its

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:55.920
internal structure. So we have a global

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:58.640
liquid water ocean underneath the

00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:01.600
surface of Enceladus. And the surface is

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:03.120
is made up mainly of water ice.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:06.720
Biggest unanswered questions for

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:09.160
Enceladus are why is there only a water

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:11.200
vapor plume at the South Pole?

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:13.440
We still don't quite understand why

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:14.800
there's a heat source.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:16.800
Because

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:18.560
the fact remains that it was formed

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:20.240
billions of years ago. Because it's so

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:21.640
small, it should have

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:24.680
cooled down.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:25.360
Um and so we think it might be linked to

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:28.000
um

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:29.360
its orbit around Saturn. So if the if

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:32.240
the orbit around the moon is not

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:33.880
completely circular, then on some parts

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.760
of the orbit it's closer to the planet

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:40.520
than on others. And when that happens,

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:40.520
tidal forces keep the interior warm.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:45.080
I think Enceladus will always be

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:50.360
memorable for me because in some ways it

