But, uh this mission would make 10 close

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:12.080
flybys of Io while orbiting uh Jupiter.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:14.320
It has a suite of instruments.

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:17.000
And the tour is designed so that we fly

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:17.760
by Io at just the right times and places

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:19.640
uh

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:21.320
in order to measure the the tidal

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:23.080
gravity signature,

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:25.760
we have to have pairs of identical

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:27.920
flybys, one when Io is closest to

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:29.960
Jupiter, and one that's otherwise

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:32.520
identical, but when Io is furthest from

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:34.040
Jupiter. So, it sees the same signal

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:36.760
except

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:39.160
that this difference in range to to

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:41.560
Jupiter, which changes the shape of of

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:43.600
Io. That'll tell us really what's going

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:45.600
on inside Io.

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:47.640
Does it really have a magma ocean? And

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:50.120
even if it has a magma ocean, what type

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:52.080
of magma ocean? That's really, I think,

00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:54.360
the most fundamental thing. We need to

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:56.800
get a spacecraft that makes close flybys

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:59.080
of of Io at a minimum.

00:24:56.800 --> 00:25:00.880
Landing on Io and orbiting Io would be

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:02.760
even better. You have to understand

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:06.360
though

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:09.120
Io is deep within the intense radiation

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:11.480
of of Jupiter. So, Jupiter has a very

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:14.080
powerful magnetosphere. So, it's a very

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:16.680
high radiation environment. It's very

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:18.280
tough on electronics.

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:19.840
So,

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:21.520
you know, we're not going to explore Io

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:23.520
the way we have Mars with these

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:25.920
long-lived rovers on the surface unless

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:27.360
we have a revolution in in electronics

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:28.720
and how to how to withstand the

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:32.280
radiation.

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:34.240
Um so, fast flybys is is the way around

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:35.880
that for now.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:37.560
Get get in there close, get a bunch of

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:40.440
data, and get out of that radiation as

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:42.960
fast as you can.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:45.000
There are volcanic plumes up to several

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:47.960
hundred kilometers high, uh very

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:51.240
spectacular. And uh

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:53.880
that some of that material may be

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:55.320
directly escape Io, but also that high

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:58.400
radiation environment and those

00:25:55.320 --> 00:26:00.840
energetic particles interact and strip

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:04.120
off material. If we're so close that

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:06.280
we're in flying through a a lava

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:08.680
fountain, that would obviously be bad

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:10.880
for a spacecraft. Uh we hope to fly

00:26:08.680 --> 00:26:12.920
through at the upper parts of plumes

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:14.680
where it's it's mostly gas and very

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:16.000
small particles that that are not

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:18.040
hazardous.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:21.280
Uh of course, if you

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:22.920
make a math error and impact Io, that

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:25.520
would not be good unless it was

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:26.920
intentional to dispose of the spacecraft

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:28.800
at the end of the mission. And that is

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:31.080
how we would plan to

00:26:28.800 --> 00:26:33.520
to end it all after the the mission is

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:33.520
over.

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:41.320
It would mean a great deal to me to see

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:42.960
this mission go forward. Now,

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:45.840
uh I'm I'm not going to be the one

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:47.760
leading the mission uh at Io, you know,

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.800
it has to be the next generation. You

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:50.520
have to think in terms of

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:52.000
multi-generations when you're dealing

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:54.000
with the outer solar system, and

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:56.240
especially when you have to spend a

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:57.760
decade just just to get the mission

00:26:56.240 --> 00:27:00.320
approved.

00:26:57.760 --> 00:27:02.680
So, for me though, that's all fine. I

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:05.840
get it started, and then I

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:07.960
enjoy life and enjoy watching

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:10.400
others carry on.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:12.600
And And I have no doubt that Io will

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:14.800
surprise us. It's It's It's a very

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:16.480
surprising place, and it's surprised

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:18.000
every mission that's ever looked at it.

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:19.760
The conventional thinking is that

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:22.800
there's no way there's life on Io. It's

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:24.320
too hot. Well, it's only hot over 1% of

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:26.240
the surface.

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:28.240
Uh there are certainly places with with

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:30.360
better temperatures. It's also thought

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:33.040
that there's no water. Well,

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:34.360
we haven't detected water,

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:36.800
but we didn't think there was water on

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:38.720
the moon either for a long time until we

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:41.360
got better measurements. So, we might be

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:42.720
surprised about that. And also, what

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:44.440
kind of life are we talking about? The

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:47.400
only thing we we know how to talk about

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:50.560
is life as we know it, which is carbon

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:52.560
and water-based life. So, if it's

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:54.960
some other kind of life, then who knows?

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:57.360
All bets are off. So, it's the surprises

00:27:54.960 --> 00:28:00.640
that will be the the most fun

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:00.640
and the most significant.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:05.920
>> I'm NASA astronaut Jessica Meir.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:09.080
I may be one of the first people to

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:09.080
return to the moon.

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:20.400
The Artemis mission has the stated goal

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:22.400
of sending the first woman and the first

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:25.400
person of color to the surface of the

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:27.680
moon. It will have been over 50 years

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:29.240
since humans have set foot there. We

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:31.360
aren't just going to go there once and

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:32.760
then come back, or go there a few times

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:35.040
like in the Apollo missions, and then

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:37.480
come back. We're going to keep this

00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:39.680
presence going with a gateway program

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:41.360
that will be an outpost in orbit around

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:43.560
the moon. Think of it kind of like a

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:45.400
little mini space station with

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:47.320
subsequent missions of landings on the

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:49.440
moon,

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:51.120
and then eventually using all of that

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:53.600
time and knowledge in those lunar

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:56.960
missions as a springboard to go even

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:58.720
further and go to Mars.

00:28:56.960 --> 00:29:00.560
My mom tells me I first started saying I

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:03.200
wanted to be an astronaut when I was 5

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:05.160
years old. So, it actually was quite

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:07.760
difficult to believe when it came true

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:10.160
in 2013 when the phone rang and I was

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:12.680
given the option to come here to NASA to

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:14.600
be an astronaut. It's almost felt

00:29:12.680 --> 00:29:15.080
surreal.

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:16.880
>> Copy catch.

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:19.240
>> Jessica is egressing. 10 seconds to a

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:21.240
20-second handover.

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:23.800
>> Coming out of the hatch for your first

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:26.600
spacewalk is a moment that I don't think

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:28.800
anyone ever forgets. My first spacewalk

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:30.640
happened to be only a few weeks after I

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:32.760
arrived on the space station. So, at

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:35.160
that point, I was still learning how to

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:38.000
move and navigate myself around on the

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:40.160
space station. That day, I was with my

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:41.880
colleague and friend Christina Koch. She

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:44.600
was in the same class of astronauts as

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:47.080
me. So, we really are like astronaut

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:48.800
siblings. She's like my space sister. We

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:51.640
were ready to go out of the hatch that

00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:52.960
day for the first all-female spacewalk.

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:54.960
Of course, there had been other women

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:57.840
that had done spacewalks before us. We

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:59.720
were the 14th and 15th women to do so.

00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:01.760
But, this was the first time that two

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:04.440
women together were going to embark on a

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:07.600
spacewalk. So, there I am and I'm

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:10.520
looking down at my feet dangling below

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:13.520
the hatch and then there is nothing else

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:15.760
between this Earth spinning below and my

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:18.840
feet. And that was just the most

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:21.520
profound moment. I was just in awe and

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:24.080
so excited. Just us up there. We're

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:27.000
floating the whole time going around our

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:29.720
planet every 90 minutes. It was still,

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:32.200
even though I was living it, even though

00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:34.080
I understood the science behind why I

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:36.920
was there, it was still difficult to

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:38.520
believe that it was actually happening.

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:40.520
So, it was really only after the

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:42.960
spacewalk ended that I started thinking

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:44.240
more about what it meant.

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:45.920
And it was interesting because in the

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:48.160
beginning I thought, "Why does it

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:50.080
matter? Why is there so much attention

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:52.560
being drawn to this first all-female

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:54.320
spacewalk?" It just happens to be the

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:56.240
the fact that right now there are two

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:58.080
women going out the door. We're just as

00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:59.960
qualified as everybody else up here. And

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:01.680
so, it's just a matter of time that this

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:04.160
would happen. But then when I started

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:07.120
thinking about it differently, so many

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:09.640
people tuned in to watch that spacewalk.

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:12.200
And so many people started giving us

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:14.440
this feedback about how inspiring it was

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:16.560
for them and how much it meant for them.

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:17.760
And that really took me a little bit by

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:19.840
surprise.

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:22.440
And I also started thinking more about

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:24.600
the generations that came before us.

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:26.400
So, Christina and I, both of us, when we

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:29.440
look at this now, we don't think about

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:31.320
it as our personal achievement. We had

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:33.120
that opportunity because of the

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:36.200
generations of women and other

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:39.200
minorities that had worked so hard, that

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:41.560
had truly pushed the envelope and broken

00:31:39.200 --> 00:31:43.680
those glass ceilings to allow us to be

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:45.320
where we were. So, now when we look

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:48.360
toward the future and we look at the

00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:49.960
Artemis program, this administration has

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:52.600
set forth the goal that we will be

00:31:49.960 --> 00:31:54.160
sending the first woman and the first

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:56.120
person of color to the surface of the

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:58.840
moon. Of course, I would be absolutely

00:31:56.120 --> 00:32:00.760
thrilled to be that person, to be one of

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:01.840
these first people returning to the

00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:04.640
moon.

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:06.480
And to me it would represent so much. I

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:08.360
know somebody asked me once what I would

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:10.760
say, what would be those those first

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:12.720
words, like we have those famous first

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:14.280
words from the Apollo mission. And that

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:16.680
is something I would have to put a lot

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:18.520
of thought into. I think it to me it

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:20.320
would be a little bit like how I

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:22.600
described that first all-female

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:24.160
spacewalk. It wouldn't be about my

00:32:22.600 --> 00:32:26.760
achievement. It would be about the

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:28.840
achievement of everybody on this NASA

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:31.200
team that got there. And it wouldn't

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:33.240
even only be limited to that. Really,

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:37.600
that next person that goes to the moon

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:40.200
is a representative of all of humanity.

00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:42.560
So, a lot of people ask us, "Why haven't

00:32:40.200 --> 00:32:45.240
we been back to the moon? If we did this

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:47.320
50 years ago, why is it so difficult?

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:49.080
Why haven't we been doing it?" There's

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:51.560
nothing easy about going to the moon.

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:54.040
What we accomplished in the '60s was

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:57.000
extraordinary. We are due to go back to

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:59.800
the moon for so many reasons. The first

00:32:57.000 --> 00:33:02.720
is simply for exploration. It is an

00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:05.360
inherent characteristic inside us as

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:07.760
humans to explore, to want to look

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:09.960
around the corner, to go further, to ask

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:12.120
those questions and just quench that

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:13.720
curiosity that we have.

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:16.760
Secondly, though, and equally as

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:18.960
important is for scientific reasons. If

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:20.840
we look back at the Apollo missions, we

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:22.520
learned so much about the our solar

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:25.200
system, about the formation of our

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:26.960
planet, and of the moon. And I know that

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:29.320
that will be true as well for the

00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:31.840
Artemis missions. For example, we'll be

00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:34.760
targeting the South Pole because at that

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:37.880
area, we know that there is much more

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:40.400
frozen water. So, by going to that spot,

00:33:37.880 --> 00:33:43.360
we can study and understand that water,

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:45.200
that ice, that frozen water will help us

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:47.840
understand more about the moon, about

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:51.120
our solar system, and it will also be a

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:52.800
resource. We can use oxygen in the soil.

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:55.560
We can use that frozen water in the

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:57.200
surface. We can use that to make fuel,

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:59.320
to make resources for further

00:33:57.200 --> 00:34:01.440
exploration. So, that would be that next

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:04.400
step. Take what we learn in the proving

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:06.320
ground around the moon, and now use that

00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:07.960
to get to Mars, where we need to be

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:10.960
autonomous. We need to think about

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:13.120
things like sustainable food systems. We

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:14.879
won't even be able to necessarily carry

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:17.080
all the food and water that we need up

00:34:14.879 --> 00:34:19.040
there. So, maybe we want to bring some

00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:21.159
things that we can grow. Everything that

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:23.480
we learn in these early Artemis missions

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:24.600
to the moon, we'll apply then on to

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:26.360
Mars.

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:28.720
Fairly soon, we will be launching that

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:30.919
Artemis 1 test mission, and that mission

00:34:28.720 --> 00:34:33.480
will be about 3 weeks. It will go all

00:34:30.919 --> 00:34:35.240
the way out to the moon and beyond it

00:34:33.480 --> 00:34:37.520
and we will test out all of those

00:34:35.240 --> 00:34:39.760
systems of the Orion spacecraft and that

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:41.840
launch system. The next mission, the

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:43.200
Artemis 2 mission, would have crew

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:45.560
members on it. So, that will be the

00:34:43.200 --> 00:34:48.040
first mission that has astronauts going

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:49.480
out to the moon. They won't yet land on

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:52.200
the surface of the moon, but they will

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:53.679
go out again further than the moon.

00:34:52.200 --> 00:34:55.480
They'll be in orbit around the moon

00:34:53.679 --> 00:34:57.360
before they come back here. So, that

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:59.200
will be another vital step in making

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:01.920
sure we have everything ready and we're

00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:03.440
prepared for those later missions which

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:04.640
will then land on the surface of the

00:35:03.440 --> 00:35:06.960
moon.

00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:10.120
We'll also have the Gateway which will

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:11.800
be in orbit around the moon. So, think

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:13.520
of it kind of like a little mini space

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:15.360
station. Instead of being around the

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:18.160
Earth though, it'll be there proximal to

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:20.520
the moon and then we can use that to go

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:22.760
back and forth. We can keep people on

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:24.640
board the Gateway and then we can have

00:35:22.760 --> 00:35:27.200
regular excursions down to the surface

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:29.160
of the moon. We can use that as a test

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:30.880
bed for other hardware and technology

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:33.080
and experiments and then we can

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:36.080
eventually use that as a stepping stone

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.080
to getting to Mars.

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:42.080
To me, the moon represents the spirit of

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:45.360
exploration that I think has driven

00:35:42.080 --> 00:35:48.960
humans from the beginning of evolution

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:51.560
of our species and also me personally.

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:54.520
The stars, the Milky Way, the moon were

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:56.680
so bright in our view and I think that's

00:35:54.520 --> 00:35:59.000
something that really shaped part of my

00:35:56.680 --> 00:36:01.480
childhood, letting that natural

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:04.480
scientific curiosity, that spirit of

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:06.200
exploration extend not only from what

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:09.440
was in front of me on the ground, but

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:10.800
also to what was lying up there above.

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:13.320
And of course, the moon is this

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:15.400
incredibly prominent object and

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:18.160
something that that I think we're all

00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:20.480
drawn to. It makes me so optimistic now

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:23.680
to know that it is a national priority

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:26.000
again. We have laid out this foundation

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:28.480
to get back to the moon. So, we will

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:28.480
return.

